# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; .env.example is not.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# A real environment variable always wins over this file, so
# `QWEN_API_KEY=other make run` still overrides it.
# Values are used literally: $OTHER is NOT expanded, and quotes are only needed
# when a value contains spaces or a '#'.

# ── which model provider ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# qwen (default) | scripted
#
# `scripted` is a fixture that replays a recorded turn: no key, no network. It
# is for tests and offline demos, not a provider.
OBA_BACKEND=qwen

# Needed when OBA_BACKEND=qwen. Create one in Model Studio at
# bailian.console.aliyun.com. There is no other credential source for this
# provider, so startup refuses without it.
#
# !! The key is REGIONAL. Model Studio's Beijing and Singapore hosts have
# separate account namespaces, and a key issued for one is rejected by the other
# with a 401 that is indistinguishable from a revoked key. If you move
# OBA_QWEN_BASE_URL you must reissue the key in the new region too.
QWEN_API_KEY=

# Override the endpoint for a proxy or the Singapore region. Default is Beijing:
#   https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1
# Singapore is https://dashscope-intl.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1 — see the
# regional warning above before changing this.
# OBA_QWEN_BASE_URL=

# Model ids default from OBA_BACKEND and rarely need setting.
#
# !! If you are upgrading from a build that used the Anthropic or DeepSeek
# backends, REMOVE any OBA_AGENT_MODEL / OBA_CLASSIFIER_MODEL left in your .env.
# Startup refuses those ids on purpose: Model Studio is a multi-vendor
# marketplace and actually answers `deepseek-v4-pro` with a 200, so a leftover
# value would keep working while billing for a model you did not choose.
# OBA_AGENT_MODEL=qwen3.8-max
# OBA_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=qwen3.8-flash

# ── how answers read ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# zh-CN (default) | en | match  — "match" mirrors whatever the person wrote in.
OBA_REPLY_LANGUAGE=zh-CN

# ── looking things up outside the corpus ────────────────────────────────────
# The official service directory ships enabled and needs nothing.
# Live web search returns current openings AND courses nationwide; it is OFF
# until a key is set, and startup logs LIVE_SEARCH_DISABLED when it is.
#
# The default vendor is bocha (博查), because these searches are for Chinese
# cities on Chinese-language sites. Set the key and nothing else.
# OBA_SEARCH_API_KEY=
#
# The two vendors answer DIFFERENT wire shapes — bocha takes a POST with a
# bearer token, brave takes a GET with a header — so the provider is named
# explicitly and never guessed from the URL. Pointing the URL at one vendor
# while the provider says the other fails SILENTLY: the request is accepted,
# nothing parses, and the city reads as empty.
# OBA_SEARCH_PROVIDER=bocha
# OBA_SEARCH_API_URL=https://api.bochaai.com/v1/web-search
#
# For brave instead, all three lines have to change together:
# OBA_SEARCH_PROVIDER=brave
# OBA_SEARCH_API_URL=https://api.search.brave.com/res/v1/web/search
# OBA_SEARCH_KEY_HEADER=X-Subscription-Token

# ── External talent scan (recruiter intent) ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Answers ONE question: how many people of a given shape exist OUTSIDE this
# service's opt-in pool. It exists because a recruiter shown three people cannot
# tell whether three is the market or just the pool, and that decides whether
# they use this service at all.
#
# ‼️ It never returns a name, an email, a phone number or a profile link — from
# either vendor, even where the vendor supplies them. Those people were never
# asked. This service will not hand over a stranger's details while refusing to
# hand over the details of somebody who opted in until they accept a job; and
# under PIPL neither vendor gives us a lawful basis to. Nobody found this way
# can be contacted through this service, and outreach_request refuses them.
#
# Both are OFF unless keyed. With neither set, the size of the outside market is
# reported as UNKNOWN — never as small — and startup logs
# EXTERNAL_TALENT_DISABLED.
#
# People Data Labs. The only evaluated vendor with a skills field, which is what
# matching here is on. Free tier: 100 lookups/month, and it WORKS on the free
# tier - unlike Apollo below.
#
# Measured China coverage on 2026-08-31, via `make talent-smoke`:
#   skills=cnc, country=china              -> 4,261 records
#   skills=cnc, worldwide                  -> 466,866
#   title=engineer, country=china          -> 266,081
#   skills=cnc AND title="cnc operator", china -> 0
# So there IS China data, and it is thin and English-titled. Match on skills, not
# on job titles, and read a zero as "not in this index" rather than as an absence
# of workers.
# OBA_PDL_API_KEY=
#
# ‼️ Point this at the SANDBOX to develop and demo against SYNTHETIC records at
# zero credit cost and with no real person's data read at all. That is the right
# endpoint for anything other than production:
# OBA_PDL_API_URL=https://sandbox.api.peopledatalabs.com/v5/person/search
#
# Apollo.io. ‼️ NOT usable on the Free plan. Its People Search costs no CREDITS,
# but access is gated by PLAN: a free-tier key is refused with
#   403 "The api/v1/mixed_people/api_search API is not included in your Free plan"
# (measured 2026-08-31). Any paid plan includes it. On a paid plan it is a good
# citizen for this use: the search response is already de-identified by the
# vendor (obfuscated surname, has_email as a boolean, not a value), and this
# adapter never calls the paid enrichment endpoints that return the values.
# OBA_APOLLO_API_KEY=
#
# What neither of them covers: both indexes are English-first and weighted to
# office work, and LinkedIn's mainland China service closed in 2023. A low count
# for manual, care or service trades says what these vendors index — NOT how
# many such workers exist. Every result carries that caveat to the reader.

# ── read-aloud ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Answers are ALWAYS readable aloud: with nothing set here the browser uses its
# own built-in voice, which needs no key and no network. These variables replace
# that with a vendor voice.
#
# Off unless keyed, deliberately. A feature that silently degrades is worse than
# one that says it is not switched on; startup logs TTS_DISABLED when there is
# no key.
#
# !! READ THIS BEFORE POINTING IT AT REAL USERS.
# The default backbone is Fish's FREE model, and Fish's published terms for it
# say requests may be used to improve model quality. The text sent is the
# ANSWER — it names the person's city, their employment situation, and often the
# benefit they are trying to claim. That is the price of the free tier, and it is
# a privacy decision, not a billing one. The paid backbones (s2.1-pro, s2-pro,
# s1) bill at $0.015 per 1,000 UTF-8 bytes, which for Chinese is 3 bytes a
# character — roughly $0.018 for a typical answer. See docs/17-read-aloud.md.
#
# OBA_TTS_API_KEY=              # fish.audio/app/api-keys — free to create
# OBA_TTS_VOICE_ID=             # the model id from the voice's fish.audio URL
# OBA_TTS_MODEL=s2.1-pro-free   # s2.1-pro-free (default, free) | s2.1-pro | s2-pro | s1
# OBA_TTS_API_URL=              # override the endpoint; defaults to Fish's

# ── everything else has a working default ───────────────────────────────────
# OBA_ADDR=:8787
# OBA_STATE_PATH=.state/oba.json
# OBA_K_ANONYMITY=5
# OBA_ENABLED_INTENTS=individual_pathway,low_access_support

# ── spending ceilings, per UTC day (0 = no ceiling) ──────────────────────────
# The budgets above bound ONE turn. These bound how many turns get run, which is
# the question that decides the model bill now that sign-up needs no invite code.
# OBA_ACCOUNT_DAILY_TOKENS bounds one account; OBA_DEPLOYMENT_DAILY_TOKENS is the
# circuit breaker across every account, because accounts are free and a
# per-account allowance alone multiplies by however many somebody registers.
# When the deployment ceiling trips, nobody is answered until 00:00 UTC.
# /api/health reports spend_today_tokens; size the ceiling from that, not a guess.
# OBA_ACCOUNT_DAILY_TOKENS=2000000
# OBA_DEPLOYMENT_DAILY_TOKENS=50000000

# ── accounts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything except /api/health and the sign-in endpoints requires an account.
# SIGN-UP IS OPEN: a username, a password and an email address create one, with
# no invite code. OBA_INVITE_CODES used to gate it and is no longer read — an
# env file that still sets it is harmless, and deleting the line is safe.
# See docs/bugfix/2026-09-01-sign-up-no-longer-needs-an-invite-code.md
# OBA_DEMO_ACCOUNT adopts subjects left behind from before accounts existed.
# OBA_DEMO_ACCOUNT=demo
# OBA_SIGNIN_TTL_DAYS=30

# ── Outgoing mail: confirm an address, and reset a forgotten password ─────────
#
# OFF unless all three of HOST, FROM and PUBLIC_ORIGIN are set. Startup logs
# MAIL_DISABLED and names the missing one. With mail off the service still signs
# people up and in — it just cannot offer a password reset, and the sign-in page
# stops offering one rather than showing a form that does nothing.
#
# 🔴 OBA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN has NO DEFAULT on purpose. Every link in an outgoing
# message is absolute against it, and a guessed origin sends mail that is either
# useless (a link to localhost) or dangerous (a link to whatever Host header a
# request carried).
# OBA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://jobs.example.com
#
# The host must be the name on the relay's CERTIFICATE — STARTTLS verifies it,
# and clear text is refused outright, because a reset link is a credential in
# transit.
# OBA_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.com
# OBA_SMTP_PORT=587
#
# FROM must be an address the credential below may send as. A relay with spoof
# protection binds a login to its own address and the aliases pointing at it, so
# "send as the support desk" is usually NOT available to a service account —
# REPLY_TO is how a person still reaches a human.
# OBA_SMTP_FROM=jobs@example.com
# OBA_SMTP_REPLY_TO=support@example.com
# OBA_SMTP_USERNAME=
# OBA_SMTP_PASSWORD=

